5:[["$","$L10","2.1",{"topic":{"id":"2.1","title":"Introduction to Cyber Crime Investigation","minutes":10,"summary":"A cyber investigation is a disciplined search for reliable answers, combining legal authority, technical examination, and human context.","sections":[{"title":"Begin with a question, not a tool","body":["An investigation starts with an allegation or incident that can be stated precisely: who experienced what harm, when, through which account or system, and with what known loss. “Hack this person” is not an investigative objective. “Determine whether the supplier mailbox was compromised and whether the changed bank instruction caused the payment” identifies testable events and evidence sources.","The investigator distinguishes incident response from evidential investigation. Containment stops ongoing harm; forensics supports reliable conclusions. They overlap but can conflict, for example when rebuilding a server destroys volatile traces. A risk-based decision, made by an authorized person and documented, is better than an automatic shutdown or an unrecorded live examination."]},{"title":"Roles and investigative authority","body":["The complainant supplies context and original records. The officer in charge directs the criminal investigation, a forensic examiner analyzes exhibits, service providers supply records within lawful authority, and prosecutors assess the proof required. A private security consultant does not automatically acquire police powers. An expert’s technical skill does not replace the legal basis for access.","In India, cyber cells, local police, and I4C-supported coordination may all be involved. The legally competent authority and jurisdiction must be identified. Specialized forensic support can be requested, but exhibit identifiers, scope, communication, and responsibility should remain clear. For urgent financial fraud, early reporting can help preserve the payment trail."]},{"title":"A reproducible investigative plan","body":["Write the initial hypotheses and alternatives. A payment may follow account compromise, a deceptive but authentic message, malware, or an internal authorization failure. For each hypothesis, list expected observations and evidence that would disprove it. Avoid confirmation bias by actively looking for the innocent explanation.","Maintain an action log with time, actor, authority, action, result, and effect on evidence. Use synchronized time references and preserve originals. The plan should be revisited when new evidence appears. A defensible final report explains not only what was found, but also what was not available, what changed, and why a conclusion is limited."]}],"practice":{"title":"Frame the first five minutes","scenario":"A finance employee reports a suspicious invoice and a transfer made ten minutes ago.","steps":["Write a neutral one-sentence investigative question.","Identify urgent loss-prevention and preservation actions.","Assign the complainant, officer, examiner, and bank contact roles.","Record two hypotheses and the evidence needed to distinguish them."],"debrief":"A focused opening prevents both delay and uncontrolled collection. Stopping further loss and preserving the original email can proceed in parallel."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bnss","it-act"],"sources":["bnss","cert","cybercrime"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":2,"objectives":["Explain begin with a question, not a tool and apply it to an investigation.","Explain roles and investigative authority and apply it to an investigation.","Explain a reproducible investigative plan and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-2.1","prompt":"What is the best first step in a cyber investigation?","options":["Run every forensic tool available.","Define the allegation, scope, authority, and urgent preservation needs.","Assume the account owner is the offender.","Delete suspicious files immediately."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A precise, authorized plan guides proportionate collection and protects the investigation from avoidable errors.","unit":2}}}]]
